C++ Employee Payroll Mini-Project
Description

This C++ program demonstrates run-time polymorphism using an employee payroll system.

The program uses an abstract Employee class as a common base class. Two derived classes, PermanentEmployee and ContractEmployee, calculate salaries differently by overriding the calculateSalary() function.

Classes Used
1. Employee Class

The Employee class is the abstract base class.

employeeId stores the employee ID.
name stores the employee's name.
The constructor initializes the employee details.
calculateSalary() is a pure virtual function.
displayBasicDetails() displays the employee ID and name.
It contains a virtual destructor.

The pure virtual function is:

virtual double calculateSalary() const = 0;

This makes Employee an abstract class.

2. PermanentEmployee Class

The PermanentEmployee class inherits from Employee.

basicSalary stores the basic salary.
allowance stores the additional allowance.
The constructor initializes the employee details and salary information.
calculateSalary() returns the basic salary plus allowance.

Formula:

Salary = Basic Salary + Allowance

For Asha:

40000 + 8000 = 48000
3. ContractEmployee Class

The ContractEmployee class also inherits from Employee.

hourlyRate stores the payment per hour.
hoursWorked stores the number of hours worked.
calculateSalary() calculates the salary based on hours worked.

Formula:

Salary = Hourly Rate × Hours Worked

For Vikas:

500 × 80 = 40000

printPaySlip() Function

The function:

void printPaySlip(const Employee& employee)

accepts a base-class reference.

It displays the employee's basic details and calls:

employee.calculateSalary();

Since calculateSalary() is virtual, the appropriate derived-class implementation is selected at run time.

Main Function

In the main() function, two employee objects are created:

Permanent Employee → ID 101, Name Asha
Contract Employee → ID 102, Name Vikas

Both objects are passed to the printPaySlip() function.

Output
Employee ID: 101
Name: Asha
Salary: Rs. 48000

Employee ID: 102
Name: Vikas
Salary: Rs. 40000
OOP Concept Used

Run-Time Polymorphism — Abstract Class and Virtual Function

                    Employee
                       |
              calculateSalary()
                       |
             +---------+---------+
             |                   |
     PermanentEmployee    ContractEmployee
             |                   |
      Basic + Allowance    Rate × Hours
             |                   |
          Rs. 48000           Rs. 40000

The Employee class provides a common interface, while each derived class provides its own salary calculation. This demonstrates run-time polymorphism using an abstract class and a pure virtual function.